  <article>
    <p class="eyebrow">Bayesian models · documentation</p>
    <h1>Four projections you can<br><em>inspect and challenge.</em></h1>
    <p class="learn-lede">Pitcher Next-Start uses four independent Bayesian models for FIP, runs, innings, and strikeouts. This page documents their shared data, separate targets, safeguards, measured performance, and limitations.</p>

    <section id="promise"><h2>1 · What it predicts</h2><p>The target is one starting pitcher’s next MLB outing. Independent Bayesian regressions produce FIP, runs allowed, expected innings, and expected strikeouts—not a generic matchup grade.</p><div class="callout"><b>One projection, two uses</b><span>The expected-runs center can feed a team model. The workload and strikeout outputs can inform prop research after comparison with a market. The app does not create an automatic bet.</span></div></section>

    <section id="pipeline"><h2>2 · Data pipeline</h2><div class="pipeline"><span>MLB schedule</span><i>→</i><span>Probable starters</span><i>→</i><span>400-day logs</span><i>→</i><span>Normalized starts</span><i>→</i><span>Features</span><i>→</i><span>Posterior</span></div><p>The reusable training file contains <strong>13,821 normalized starts from 2023–2025</strong>. MLB Stats API supplies schedules, game logs, handedness, and team splits. Baseball Savant supplies four-seam and sinker velocity. Raw responses never enter the model directly.</p></section>

    <section id="features"><h2>3 · What the models see</h2><p>All four models use the same leakage-safe feature row. Rolling form uses at most the five starts before the target date. Missing optional measurements fall back to neutral values rather than dropping the pitcher.</p><div class="learn-table"><div class="table-head"><span>Feature</span><span>Meaning</span><span>Card display</span></div><div><b>K% / BB%</b><span>Strikeouts and walks per batter faced</span><span>Recent form</span></div><div><b>HR/9, WHIP, ER/9</b><span>Contact damage and run prevention</span><span>Recent form</span></div><div><b>GB%</b><span>Ground outs divided by ground + air outs</span><span>Pitch shape</span></div><div><b>Fastball velocity</b><span>Average FF/SI release speed by start</span><span>Pitch shape</span></div><div><b>Rest and home/away</b><span>Days since previous start and venue side</span><span>Context chips</span></div><div><b>Park factor</b><span>Prior-date run environment, shrunk to league</span><span>1.00 = neutral</span></div><div><b>Opponent factor</b><span>Prior-date opponent production vs pitcher hand</span><span>1.00 = neutral</span></div></div></section>

    <section id="model"><h2>4 · Four Bayesian linear models</h2><div class="formula">yᵢ,target = β₀,target + xᵢᵀβtarget + εᵢ,target<br><span>target ∈ &#123; FIP, runs, innings, strikeouts &#125;</span></div><p>Each target has its own coefficients, posterior, prior intercept, and observation noise. The models share the same leakage-safe feature rows, but one output is never calculated from another.</p></section>

    <section id="priors"><h2>5 · Priors and standardization</h2><p>Continuous features are standardized from the training rows before fitting. Coefficients receive independent Normal priors with variance 4.0. Intercept priors center at 4.20 FIP, 3.0 runs, 5.3 innings, and 5.2 strikeouts. Runs and FIP use 2.25 observation-noise variance; innings uses 1.0 and strikeouts uses 6.25.</p><div class="callout muted"><b>Why standardize?</b><span>K rate, velocity, WHIP, and rest live on different scales. Standardization makes the same prior strength interpretable across features and prevents large-number columns from dominating the matrix.</span></div></section>

    <section id="posterior"><h2>6 · Posterior → projection card</h2><div class="learn-table compact"><div class="table-head"><span>Card field</span><span>Model source</span><span>Interpretation</span></div><div><b>Projected FIP</b><span>Posterior predictive mean</span><span>Central skill estimate</span></div><div><b>Runs low/base/high</b><span>Calibrated predictive interval</span><span>Plausible outing range</span></div><div><b>Expected innings</b><span>Independent workload posterior</span><span>Central workload estimate</span></div><div><b>Expected strikeouts</b><span>Independent strikeout posterior</span><span>Central strikeout estimate</span></div><div><b>Confidence</b><span>Walk-forward uncertainty tier</span><span>Reliability label, not quality</span></div><div><b>Model read</b><span>Deterministic template</span><span>No generated “AI score”</span></div></div></section>

    <section id="leakage"><h2>7 · Leakage controls</h2><p>Every historical feature row at date t uses starts strictly before t. Context enrichment processes an entire date before updating opponent or park totals, so another game on the same date cannot leak into the row. Walk-forward models train on earlier dates and score later dates.</p><div class="rule-list"><span><b>01</b> Prior starts only</span><span><b>02</b> Same-date isolation</span><span><b>03</b> Chronological validation</span><span><b>04</b> Normalized tables only</span></div></section>

    <section id="validation"><h2>8 · Measured validation</h2><p>The current report holds out <strong>2,775 starts across 113 future dates</strong>. Lower MAE is better.</p><div class="score-table"><div><span>Method</span><span>FIP MAE</span></div><div class="winner"><b>Bayesian model</b><strong>2.054</strong></div><div><b>Pitcher-history average</b><strong>2.074</strong></div><div><b>League average</b><strong>2.088</strong></div><div><b>Last-five average</b><strong>2.226</strong></div></div><p class="fine-print">The model beats every listed baseline, but only narrowly. Runs MAE is 1.569. This is evidence of incremental signal—not evidence of a durable betting edge.</p></section>

    <section id="calibration"><h2>9 · Interval and confidence calibration</h2><p>The raw nominal 90% run band covered only 81.2% of held-out results, meaning it was overconfident. The app measures standardized walk-forward residuals and applies a 1.134 width multiplier. Calibrated coverage is 90.0%.</p><p>Confidence separates reducible parameter uncertainty from the outing noise used in the run band, normalizes for training-set size, then penalizes samples shorter than five starts. Five-start predictions from the earlier 70% of walk-forward dates define candidate tiers; the later 30% audit realized run error. Three tiers activate only when each has at least 100 audit starts and MAE improves by at least 5% at each step. Otherwise the app withholds high confidence.</p><div class="coverage"><div><span>Raw coverage</span><b>81.2%</b></div><i>→</i><div class="good"><span>Calibrated</span><b>90.0%</b></div></div></section>

    <section id="example"><h2>10 · Worked card</h2><div class="example-card"><div><span>Recent five</span><b>27.4% K · 7.8% BB · 1.08 WHIP</b></div><div><span>Shape</span><b>94.8 mph FB · 46% GB</b></div><div><span>Context</span><b>6 days rest · park 0.97 · opponent 1.04</b></div><hr><div><span>Posterior mean</span><strong>3.72 FIP</strong></div><div><span>Expected runs</span><strong>0.4 / 2.3 / 5.0</strong></div></div><p>The context is slightly mixed: a pitcher-friendly park but an opponent above the handedness-adjusted league average. The wide run band matters more than a few hundredths of projected FIP.</p></section>

    <section id="use"><h2>11 · How to use the card</h2><p><strong>For a team model:</strong> pass expected-runs base as the starter input and preserve low/high for scenario analysis. <strong>For props:</strong> compare the full distribution with the offered line and price. No market comparison means no bet conclusion.</p></section>

    <section id="limits"><h2>12 · Limits and next work</h2><ul><li>Park context is a home-team park proxy, not a weather-adjusted physical-venue model.</li><li>Opponent context is public-data run production, not projected batting-order quality.</li><li>The regression is not hierarchical across pitchers, teams, or seasons.</li><li>Velocity covers 99.9% of processed starts; missing measurements use a neutral fallback.</li><li>FIP MAE remains high in absolute terms, and the baseline advantage is small.</li><li>Live odds, vig, bankroll, and full-game win probability are outside this model.</li></ul></section>
  </article>
</main>
